SWEET Bluetooth: LE-state probe matrix + HCI trace cap 200 -> 3000 (patch 0216, ticket T-0c77, W-BTUP; boot-arg sweetbtleprobe=1 gates the matrix, the trace cap applies to every sweetbt=1 boot). bt-up4 [obs 20261104T032008Z-bt-up4]: the unbooted BCM4389 ROM answers 67 of bluetoothd's commands but returns 0x0c (Command Disallowed) to LE Read Advertising Channel TX Power (0x2007) and LE Set Scan Parameters (0x200b); bluetoothd exits on the 0x200b status (scan completion handler 0x1005798d4 [read from source]). The matrix runs once after the baud probe settled, before the rx thread and bluetoothd, then power-cycles the controller and re-probes the baud so bluetoothd meets a clean ROM. Phase B replays bluetoothd's own LE commands of the bt-up4 trace in order (2003 2001 0c6d 2060 202f 2031 2024 201c 200f 204a 2074) with 0x2007 after every step (names the first step after which the ROM says 0x0c; Opus review T-0c77: 0x204a is an LE 5.0 command sent before 0x2007), then the triple (2007; 200b with bluetoothd's parameters, own address random; 200b own address public). Phase A resets and adds single preconditions each followed by the triple: LE Set Scan Enable off 0x200c 00 00, LE Set Random Address 0x2005, Write BD_ADDR 0xfc01 plen 6 [Android HAL + Linux btbcm.c:101-116, docs/research/BT-S1-android-oracle.md row 7], HCI Reset, plus BD address reads and the Supported Commands octets 24..30. Controller writes: standard HCI commands that bluetoothd or the Android HAL send themselves, volatile controller RAM state that REG_ON low clears (the power-cycle at the end), no flash, no patchram, no baud change, no scan or advertising enable (a test asserts it). The trace cap rises to 3000 transfers because bt-up4 hit 200 inside the 2nd respawn. Applies after 0189, 0213 and 0215. Not run on hardware yet.

